11.0.0 — effects that play late glide in and out while the song plays; the Apple Health texts corrected

What is new since 10076. Each line is code that the Echoel Studio run builds. The engine's part of
the first six lines has tests on audio tracks: stand-in effects for every line, the Multiband
Clipper's own signal code for On, Off and a swap, and the Tape's for On and for taking it off again
before or during its glide in. On a return only a stand-in arriving is tested, on the master only
the Clipper arriving; Off or Remove there has no test. An instrument track goes through the same
code and has no test of its own. Tape and Clipper are tested as their signal code, not as the Audio
Units the app hosts them in; Pitch Correction and plug-ins of other makers are in no crossover test.
The app's part, which turns On, Off, Add Effect and Remove into those changes, has no test. The
Learn sentence's opening words and its 2 minutes have a test; the two menu captions have none.
Nothing here is device-verified yet.
- Effects that play late (Tape, 6 ms; Pitch Correction, 19 ms; the Multiband Clipper, 85 samples;
  another maker's plug-in that reports its delay): one switched On or Off, added or removed while
  the song plays now crosses over. In 10076, when one came in, each track that waits for it fell
  silent for the wait and came back with a hard edge. Its own track fell silent for the effect's
  delay and came in with a step. One taken off stopped at once, and the other tracks skipped what
  they still held.
- On, or Add Effect once the effect has loaded (its row reads "Loading…" until then): every track
  first plays on as it was, the new effect running unheard, for the effect's delay and as much
  again (the second part a tenth of a second at most). At 48 kHz that is 12 ms with Tape, 38 ms
  with Pitch Correction and 3.5 ms with the Multiband Clipper. Then all tracks glide together
  over 256 samples (5.3 ms at 48 kHz): the effect's track into the effect's sound, the others
  into waiting for it.
- Off, or Remove, of the only effect that plays late: no wait. From the next audio buffer all tracks
  glide over the same 256 samples, the effect's track to its sound without the effect, the others
  out of their wait; the effect runs on, in time with the song, until the glide is over. With
  another such effect on another track, every track first plays on as it was for as much time as a
  track's wait grows by (6 ms when Tape or Pitch Correction goes Off while the other stays), then
  the tracks whose wait changes glide.
- The same on an instrument track, an audio track, a return and the master (the Effects key of
  any Mixer strip).
- An edit made while a crossover runs: before the glide the engine takes it at once (a fader, a
  mute, a note drawn in a clip; a key you play is heard at once, glide or not). During the 256
  samples of the glide it takes none; such an edit is heard when the glide is over, about 5 ms and
  one audio buffer later at most.
- Two edits that reach the engine inside one audio buffer (an effect that plays late switched and a
  fader moved, say) are taken as one change, worked out against what is playing (the fader at once,
  the effect with its crossover); the state after the first alone is never played. In 10076 the
  second was built on the first, which the engine skipped, and a track that waits could fall silent
  for its whole wait in the middle of the song.
- Unchanged: an effect that reports no delay (EQ, Compressor, Reverb, Delay and the rest) still
  switches at once when it is turned On or Off, added or removed, and Move Up and Move Down
  still switch at once, also for effects that play late. An export has no crossover and starts
  on the song's first sample, as in 10076. An effect switched while the song is stopped crosses
  over in silence, and Play then starts in time.
- Body Source menu (Song menu › Connect › Body Source): the caption under "Apple Health (Apple
  Watch)" now reads "Arrives late, whenever Health syncs: slow modulation only, never the beat. Read
  only; the reading stays on this device." It said "Arrives 4–5 s late" and "stays on this iPhone".
  The caption under "Phone Motion" says "this device" where it said "this iPhone".
- Learn, chapter "Modulation and Body Sources": the sentence on Apple Health now reads "Apple Health
  arrives late: a Watch reading comes only when Health syncs, and Apple gives no figure for how long
  that takes; a reading older than 2 minutes moves nothing."

Known limit, unchanged since 10074: a MIDI take does not record a key held across the loop's end
as it was heard. While the record line shows "Loop Record · take n" (a loop of two bars or
longer), the note ends at the loop's end and the next take does not have it. On a one-bar loop, a
key let go after the loop came round is recorded shorter than it was held. ("take n" stands for
the number the line shows: take 1, take 2 …)
Known limit, unchanged since 10076: while an effect that plays late is on a track (even a muted
one), on the master, or on a return a send feeds (Tape 6 ms, Pitch Correction 19 ms, the
Multiband Clipper 85 samples), the whole mix plays that much behind the click, the Video Window's
picture and what goes out as MIDI, and an audio or MIDI take played along with the tracks instead
of the click lands that much late.
Known limit, as old as that lag and first stated here: each time such an effect comes or goes while
the song plays, the mix moves against the click, the picture and outgoing MIDI by as much as that
lag changes. That is the effect's whole delay when nothing else in the song plays late, and nothing
when Tape goes Off while Pitch Correction stays on another track. When it comes in, that much of the
song is heard a second time; when it goes, that much is passed over. In 10076 it moved with a gap or
a step, now under a glide of about 5 ms; it still moves. With a plug-in that reports a long delay
that is up to one second.
Known limit, new: in the 256 samples of a glide a sound meets a copy of itself as far away as the
glide moves it. When nothing else in the song plays late, that is one delay. On the effect's own
strip (its track or return; for an effect on the master, the whole mix) the sound without the effect
is faded into the effect's sound, which comes one delay later (On), or back (Off). Each track that
waits is faded into its own sound one delay later (On) or earlier (Off). Beside another such effect,
a track glides by as much as its wait changes (13 ms when Pitch Correction goes Off while Tape
stays), or not at all. A held note can thin out or dip for those 5 ms, and a beat inside the glide
can sound doubled when the effect comes in, or softer when it goes. On a track that waits, a partial
with an odd number of half periods in that distance (with Tape alone 83 Hz, 250 Hz, 417 Hz …) passes
through silence in the middle.
Known limit, new: with effects that play late on two tracks, a track whose own such effect comes or
goes glides twice if it still waits afterwards (Pitch Correction on one track, then Tape On on
another; or, with both on, Pitch Correction Off). The second glide follows the first by its new wait
(13 ms in the first example, 6 ms in the second); from the one to the other it plays out of line
with the rest by the delay of the effect that came or went (6 ms with Tape, 19 ms with Pitch
Correction). On that track the effect itself comes in, or goes, with the second glide. No step and
no gap.
Known limit, new: in a quick run of edits to one track's effects, a second edit that lands
before the first one's glide is over, and is not the first one taken back, can fall back to the
switch of 10076 for that track's effects (one starts with its delay of silence, or stops at
once); the other tracks still glide. By hand this takes two edits within about 5 to 43 ms and
one audio buffer with Echoelmusic's own effects (the short end after an Off), or within the
1.1 s of the plug-in below.
Known limit, new: a plug-in of another maker that reports a long delay is not heard for that delay
and up to a tenth of a second more after On, or after it has loaded: 1.1 s for one that reports a
second. Until then On or Add Effect seems to do nothing; taken off again in that time, it was never
heard and nothing changes. Taken off while another such plug-in stays on another track, it plays on
for up to a second before the glide. Unchanged: a reported delay of more than one second is made up
for one second only.
Known limit, unchanged since 10076: when the screen turns sideways (Portrait Orientation Lock
off), the take camera's preview stays a tall box and shows the picture on its side; the take
itself is written upright.
Known limits of saving, older than this build and first stated here, for checks 3 and 4. A song
swiped away in the App Switcher within ten seconds of the app's start, before its audio files have
loaded, is taken for a start that did not finish: the next start opens a new song and says so, and
the song opens when it is chosen under Song menu → Open. A start opens the song whose file was
written last, not the one that was open when the app closed. An effect's values stay in the effect
until the song takes them, which it does, among other moments, when the app leaves the front (the
App Switcher counts), when another song opens and at an export; for another maker's plug-in also
when its page closes or a preset is chosen. The save two seconds after an edit does not read them,
so if the app stops by itself while in front, effect values moved since then are not in the song.
Known limits on an iPad, older than this build and first stated here, for check 5: Echoelmusic is
one build for iPhone and iPad (iOS or iPadOS 18 or later). The automatic start check runs it on an
iPhone simulator only; it compiles the build for the iPad and has never started it on one. The Sky
follows the device as if it were held upright: with the screen turned sideways the drawn sky is a
quarter turn off (the horizon stands on end) until a drag on the sky turns it by finger. On an iPad
some texts still say "phone", among them the Body Source entry "Phone Motion", the notes on the Jam
and Go Live pages, two chapter summaries in Learn, the camera pulse's "No light" hint and the system
question for the local network.

Previous: 11.0.0 (10076) — video takes that hold the song, the screen and the beat; the Tape
effect in time; exports that start on the bar; 10075 — the top bar rebuilt as a deck and a
display on one grid. Earlier: git history.

Bump this file on a commit whose "Echoel Studio" run is green to upload a build
(.github/workflows/studio-testflight.yml). Build number = 10000 + the workflow's run number.
The version is MARKETING_VERSION in EchoelStudio/project.yml.
This file is a test list for the device, not a source for claims (ContentPipeline/CLAIMS.md).

Five things to try on the device (answer each yes or no). Checks 1 and 2 use one song: a synth
track on the Init sound or Warm Pad, its Arpeggiator off, whose clip holds long notes (drawn, or
played in on the screen keys), and an audio track with a loop; both sound all the time. Do
check 1 first: it ends with Tape removed, and check 2 needs a song with no effect that plays
late. Check 5 needs an iPad.
1. Click off (the metronome key; on a phone, More (…) › Click), Loop on. Press Play and leave the
   song playing. Mixer → the audio track's Effects → Add Effect → Tape. Four changes follow, about
   two seconds apart: Tape comes in by itself when its row stops reading "Loading…"; then tap On
   beside it (it then reads Off); tap it again (On); then … beside it › Remove. Each of the four
   times: does the loop go into the Tape sound or out of it without a click or a gap, and do the
   synth's long notes play on without a click or a gap? (Each time the mix moves by 6 ms, and for
   about 5 ms the loop and a held note are each mixed with a copy of themselves 6 ms away: a soft
   dip, a change of tone, or a beat that sounds doubled when Tape comes in or softer when it goes,
   is the glide; say so if you hear it. A click, a crackle or silence is a no; say which track and
   which of the four changes.)
2. The same song, Tape removed, Click off, Loop on. Before Play, in Arrange: touch and hold the
   audio track's name › Automate › its Volume, then tap the new lane about halfway down at the
   song's end (the level now falls along the song and jumps back where the loop starts again);
   hold the name again › Modulate › its Pan › "Bar LFO → …". Press Play and let the song come
   round twice. While it plays: on the screen keys, hold a key the clip never plays across the
   loop's end, then let go; drag the synth's fader in the Mixer; tap Arrange's ruler at another
   bar (with a MIDI keyboard, hold a key through that tap). Then press Stop, tap the ruler over
   the middle of the audio loop and press Play. Is there no click, no crackle and no gap
   anywhere, and does the held key sound on through the loop's end (and, from a MIDI keyboard,
   through the tap on the ruler) and stop when you let go? (For a no, say where.)
3. In a short song (the one of checks 1 and 2 will do), record an audio take of a few seconds (arm
   an audio track, press Record, press Record again to end it) and wait until it shows on the track
   in Arrange. Rename the song (Song menu → Song Settings…, a name no other song has, Done), swipe
   the app away in the App Switcher and open it again, press Play and look under Song menu → Open.
   Then swipe it away, open it, swipe it away again within about two seconds of the song showing,
   and open it once more. After the first reopening, did the same song open under its new name, did
   the take play, and did Song menu → Open list the song once, under the new name and no longer
   under the old one; and after the last, did the same song open again, with no line saying that
   Echoelmusic did not finish starting last time? (For a no, say which part. If that line does show,
   say how many seconds passed before the swipe; the song is then under Song menu → Open.)
4. On a track that plays, add Delay (Mixer → Effects → Add Effect → Delay), tap Time, type 500
   and OK; if a plug-in of another maker is installed and its page shows a Preset menu with
   presets, put it on a track too and choose one of them. Tap Done, open another song (Song menu
   → Open; with no other song, Song menu → New Song → Empty) and then this one again. In the
   track's Effects tap Delay, tap Ping-Pong so it reads On, then swipe the app away in the App
   Switcher and open it again. Both times (tap Delay in the track's Effects to see its values):
   does Delay show what you set, Time 500 ms and the second time also Ping-Pong On, and does the
   plug-in play the preset you chose? (For a no, say which value and which time.)
5. On an iPad at full screen (iPadOS 18 or later, rotation lock off), in the song the app opens
   with (a first install opens Four Tracks): tap the seven views on the bottom bar from left to
   right and do one thing in each. Instrument: play a key or a pad. Tracks: Add Track › Audio.
   Arrange: with the song stopped, tap the ruler near bar 3. Session: Add Scene. Mixer: drag the
   Master fader. Field: the full-screen key at the top right, then the key in the same corner to
   come back. Sky: choose a city in the place menu at the top left (the first time, iPadOS asks
   for your location; either answer will do). Then turn the iPad once. Does the app start on the
   iPad, can every view be operated (a note, a new track, the position near bar 3, a new scene
   row, the number under the fader following, the Field filling the screen and coming back, a
   sky drawn), and is every control of the top bar in the bar or under More (…), nothing
   overlapping, upright and sideways? For a no, say which view. (Expected: − and + beside the
   tempo only where the bar is wide; the count-in under a held Click or under More (…); a long
   song name ending in … is cut on purpose; sideways the Sky is drawn on its side until you drag
   it.) With no iPad on iPadOS 18 or later at hand, answer "no iPad", not yes, and judge only
   the top bar, on an iPhone held sideways with Portrait Orientation Lock off.
